## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to structure a tiny Terraform project with proper state backends, a reusable module, and basic CI guards.

## Key Concepts
- Local vs remote backends and state isolation
- Modules and outputs for reusability
- State locking with DynamoDB
- CI gates that validate plan scope

## Code Example
```hcl
# backend for dev (local)
terraform {
  backend "local" {
    path = "terraform.tfstate"
  }
}
```

```hcl
# modules/storage/main.tf
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  bucket = var.bucket_name
  acl    = "private"
  versioning {
    enabled = var.versioning
  }
}

resource "aws_dynamodb_table" "lock" {
  name           = "tfstate-lock"
  billing_mode   = "PAY_PER_REQUEST"
  hash_key       = "LockID"
  attribute {
    name = "LockID"
    type = "S"
  }
}

output "bucket_name" {
  value = aws_s3_bucket.bucket.bucket
}

output "locks_table_arn" {
  value = aws_dynamodb_table.lock.arn
}
```

```hcl
# modules/storage/variables.tf
variable "bucket_name" { type = string }
variable "versioning" { type = bool; default = true }
```

```hcl
# backend for prod (example)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket         = "tfstate-prod"
    key            = "path/to/env/terraform.tfstate"
    region         = "us-east-1"
    dynamodb_table = "tfstate-lock"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend the module to support versioned backups with lifecycle rules?
- How would you add a simple test to ensure only module-controlled attributes change during plan?

## Why This Is Asked
This assesses practical multi-cloud Terraform coordination, provider aliasing, per-cloud backends, and gating across tenants.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-cloud provider configuration with aliasing
- Per-cloud backends and remote state isolation
- CI gates that validate plans across clouds
- Enforcing region constraints and mandatory tagging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton Terraform-like snippet demonstrating provider aliases and cloud map
variable "cloud" { type = string; description = "aws or gcp" }

provider "aws" {
  region = lookup(var.cloud_map, var.cloud, {})["aws_region"]
}
provider "google" {
  region = lookup(var.cloud_map, var.cloud, {})["gcp_region"]
}

module "tenant_network" {
  source = "./modules/network"
  cloud  = var.cloud
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale adding additional clouds?
- How would you verify tagging and cost-center propagation across tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical module design and basic policy; it checks input validation, derived values, and CI gates.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform module input validation
- Local/derived values
- Regex-based naming conventions
- Basic GitHub Actions gates

## Code Example
```terraform
variable "env" {
  type = string
  description = "Environment: dev|staging|prod"
  validation {
    condition = contains(["dev","staging","prod"], var.env)
    error_message = "env must be dev, staging, or prod"
  }
}

variable "bucket_name" {
  type    = string
  default = ""
}

resource "random_id" "suffix" { byte_length = 4 }

locals {
  derived_bucket = var.bucket_name != "" ? var.bucket_name : "${var.env}-app-${random_id.suffix.hex}"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  bucket = local.derived_bucket
  versioning {
    enabled = true
  }
  server_side_encryption_configuration {
    rule {
      apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
        sse_algorithm = "AES256"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you integrate this with a per-env backend and a separate plan stage for prod-only changes?
- How would you extend validation to enforce unique bucket names across multiple regions?
